# What the release workflow bakes into every CLI binary it builds and what it
# expects of the Windows signature. Not a secret: a reviewable, committed
# statement (agentry-cli#196; plan cli-release-signing.md; the desktop's
# build/release.env is the same convention).
#
# The registry a downloaded binary talks to when the person configures
# nothing, one per channel (agentry-cli#240, DR-33). This file is the same on
# main and dev; scripts/release-registry.sh picks the line by the branch the
# release is cut from: a commit that has been the head of main bakes
# production's, one that has been the head of dev bakes dev's. Each site's
# download page refuses a build that bakes the other's.
AGENTRY_REGISTRY_PRODUCTION=https://secureagentry.net
AGENTRY_REGISTRY_DEV=https://dev.secureagentry.net
# The CN the Windows signature must carry: the name Azure Artifact Signing
# validated (plan os-code-signing.md D1). scripts/check-windows-binary.sh
# refuses a release signed by any other name — the root it chains to is
# every Artifact Signing customer's, so the name is what makes it ours.
AGENTRY_WINDOWS_SIGNING_CN="Chris Dupres"
# The macOS Developer ID identity and its Team ID (agentry-cli#198; the
# desktop's agentry-desktop#152 certificate, plan cli-release-signing.md D6).
# Public: both are in every signature. scripts/sign-darwin.sh refuses a
# signature that carries any other identity or team.
APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY="Developer ID Application: Chris Dupres (5K7DTJ5AA5)"
APPLE_TEAM_ID=5K7DTJ5AA5
